Subnetting (Computer Networks)

Definition:
Subnetting is the process of dividing a large IP network into smaller logical networks called subnets. It helps in efficient use of IP addresses and improves network performance and management.

Why subnetting is needed:

Efficient utilization of IP addresses

Reduction of network congestion and broadcast traffic

Improved security through network isolation

Easier network administration

Better routing efficiency

Basic concepts

IP address:
An IPv4 address consists of 32 bits divided into two parts:

Network portion

Host portion

Example: 192.168.1.10

Subnet mask:
A subnet mask identifies the network and host parts of an IP address.
Example: 255.255.255.0 corresponds to /24.

CIDR notation:
CIDR represents the subnet mask using prefix length.
Example: /24 means 24 bits for network and 8 bits for host.

Subnetting formulas:
Number of subnets = 2^n
Number of hosts per subnet = 2^h − 2

Where:
n = number of borrowed bits
h = number of remaining host bits

Subnetting example:
Given network: 192.168.1.0/24
Required subnets: 4

To create 4 subnets, borrow 2 bits (2^2 = 4).
New subnet mask = /26 (255.255.255.192)

Subnets formed:

192.168.1.0 – 192.168.1.63

192.168.1.64 – 192.168.1.127

192.168.1.128 – 192.168.1.191

192.168.1.192 – 192.168.1.255

Each subnet supports 62 hosts.

Advantages of subnetting:

Reduces broadcast traffic

Improves network performance

Enhances security

Efficient IP allocation

Enables hierarchical routing

